---
import Article from "../../../layouts/Article.astro";
import KeyTakeaway from "../../../components/KeyTakeaway.astro";
import InfoBox from "../../../components/InfoBox.astro";
import StepList from "../../../components/StepList.astro";
import Step from "../../../components/Step.astro";
import QuickFact from "../../../components/QuickFact.astro";
---

<Article
  title="How to Play Cascadia: Complete Rules for New Players"
  description="Tile and token drafting, placement rules, and the two ways a turn can go wrong."
  game="cascadia"
  gameTitle="Cascadia"
  category="Rules"
  product="cascadia-pinecone-tokens"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    Cascadia has one of the shortest rulebooks of any Spiel des Jahres winner.
    You take a tile, you take an animal, you place both. That's the game. The
    depth is entirely in <em>where</em>, and this guide covers everything you need
    before your first play.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="On your turn take one of four tile-and-token pairs, add the tile to your landscape, and place the animal on a matching slot. Score wildlife patterns, habitat corridors and leftover nature tokens at the end." />

  <div class="my-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <QuickFact label="Players" value="1–4" icon="👥" />
    <QuickFact label="Time" value="30–45 min" icon="⏱️" />
    <QuickFact label="Turns each" value="20" icon="🔁" />
    <QuickFact label="Award" value="Spiel des Jahres 2022" icon="🏆" />
  </div>

  <h2>The Two Halves of the Game</h2>

  <p>
    Everything in Cascadia is either a <strong>habitat</strong> or a
    <strong>wildlife</strong>. Keeping them straight in your head makes the rest
    of the rules obvious.
  </p>

  <div class="my-6 grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
    <div class="p-5 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark mb-3">🏔️ Five habitats (on tiles)</h4>
      <ul class="text-sm text-bark/60 space-y-1">
        <li>• Mountains</li>
        <li>• Forests</li>
        <li>• Prairies</li>
        <li>• Wetlands</li>
        <li>• Rivers</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="p-5 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark mb-3">🐻 Five wildlife (tokens)</h4>
      <ul class="text-sm text-bark/60 space-y-1">
        <li>• Bear</li>
        <li>• Elk</li>
        <li>• Salmon</li>
        <li>• Hawk</li>
        <li>• Fox</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Setup</h2>

  <StepList title="Getting to the first turn">
    <Step number={1} title="Give everyone a starter">
      Each player takes a three-tile starter arrangement and places it in front of
      them. This is the seed of your landscape.
    </Step>
    <Step number={2} title="Draw one scoring card per animal">
      There are several scoring card variants for each of the five animals. Draw
      one for each — this combination defines how wildlife scores <em>this game</em>,
      and it changes every session.
    </Step>
    <Step number={3} title="Build the display">
      Lay out four habitat tiles in a row and draw four wildlife tokens from the
      bag, pairing one token with each tile.
    </Step>
    <Step number={4} title="Nature tokens to one side">
      Keep the pinecone-shaped nature tokens in a shared supply. You'll earn them
      during play.
    </Step>
  </StepList>

  <h2>Taking a Turn</h2>

  <p>
    Turns are fast and always the same shape.
  </p>

  <StepList title="Your turn, every turn">
    <Step number={1} title="Choose a pair">
      Take one of the four tile-and-token pairs from the display. You take
      <strong>both</strong> — the tile and the animal paired with it.
    </Step>
    <Step number={2} title="Place the tile">
      Add it to your landscape so it touches at least one tile you already have.
      There is no grid to fill and no shape to respect — your landscape grows
      organically.
    </Step>
    <Step number={3} title="Place the animal">
      Every tile shows one to three wildlife symbols. Put your token on any empty
      tile in your landscape whose symbols include that animal. It doesn't have to
      be the tile you just placed.
    </Step>
    <Step number={4} title="Refill the display">
      Draw a new tile and a new token to bring the display back to four pairs.
    </Step>
  </StepList>

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="One token per tile, forever">
    A tile can hold at most one wildlife token, and once placed a token never
    moves. Every placement is permanent, which is why Cascadia rewards thinking
    two turns ahead rather than five.
  </InfoBox>

  <InfoBox type="warning" title="If you can't place the animal, discard it">
    Sometimes no tile in your landscape accepts the animal you drafted. That's
    legal — you simply discard the token and keep the tile. It hurts, and avoiding
    that situation is most of what good Cascadia play consists of.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Nature Tokens</h2>

  <p>
    Some tiles are <strong>keystone tiles</strong> — they show a single wildlife
    symbol. Place the matching animal on one and you immediately earn a nature
    token.
  </p>

  <p>Spend a nature token at any point on your turn to do one of two things:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Break the pairing.</strong> Take any tile from the display with any token from the display, in whatever combination you like.</li>
    <li><strong>Cull the wildlife.</strong> Discard any number of tokens from the display and draw replacements.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Each unspent nature token is worth one point at the end, so every spend is a
    real trade-off. <a href="/games/cascadia/cascadia-nature-tokens-guide/">When to spend them</a>
    is one of the genuine skills of the game.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="Overpopulation">
    If all four tokens in the display show the same animal, return them to the bag
    and draw four new ones — nobody chooses, it just happens. If exactly three
    match, any player may cull those three for free at the start of their turn
    before drafting.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Ending the Game and Scoring</h2>

  <p>
    The game ends after each player has taken 20 turns. Then you score three
    things:
  </p>

  <div class="my-6 space-y-3">
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">WILDLIFE</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Score each animal by its card</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Every animal scores by the pattern on the card drawn during setup — pairs, lines, groups, or positions relative to other animals. This is usually the bigger half of your score. <a href="/games/cascadia/cascadia-scoring-cards-explained/">All five explained here</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">HABITAT</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Largest corridor of each habitat</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">For each of the five habitat types, count your biggest connected group of that terrain. You score points for its size, plus a bonus if yours is the largest at the table.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-sky-100 text-sky-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">NATURE</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">One point per unspent token</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Straightforward, and frequently the margin in a close game.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="The mistake every first-time player makes">
    Chasing animals and ignoring terrain. Habitat corridors are quiet points that
    accumulate without you noticing, and the largest-corridor bonuses swing games.
    Place tiles for terrain continuity first, then find a home for the animal.
  </InfoBox>
</Article>
